{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+3xy,\\,y(-2)=-1\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and the current value $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{\\text{new}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{\\text{new}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =y+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ 3{y}_{\\text{new}}(1-{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =3y+2{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(3-3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =3y+2{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{3-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-333415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{3+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{3-3y}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{3-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{\\text{new}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}y"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{\\text{new}}-y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{3y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{3-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-{x}^{2}+3xy,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=1.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and the current value $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-333421" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333421" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{1+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{1-3y}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}y"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-3y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333421" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2}y,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and the current value $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) & =y+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ 5{y}_{\\text{new}}(1+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) & =5y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(5+5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) & =5y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{5+5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333423" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-333423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{5+5y}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{5+5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{5-5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-5y"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333423" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{5+5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-325169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}-3xy,\\,y(-2)=3\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=1.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and the current value $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-325169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1+3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-325169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-325169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-325169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1+3y}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3y"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3{x}_{\\text{new}}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-325169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1)y+2x,\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and the current value $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2{x}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{5}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) & =y+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ 5{y}_{\\text{new}}(\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) & =5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(4-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) & =5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{4-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-333648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{4+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{4-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{4-{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+\\frac{1}{5}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)y"] ["math" "{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+\\frac{1}{5}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)y+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-333648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{4-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+2)y+{e}^{x},\\,y(1)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(2)≈$ {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =((({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2){y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2){y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2){y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ (1-(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}+{e}^{2}}{1+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}+{e}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & ≈-1.58,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-1.58.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-339698" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339698" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.64"] ["math" "-1.05"] ["math" "-1.58"] ["math" "4.72"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339698" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.58"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(2{e}^{x}+1),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}_{\\text{new}}(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1) & =y \\\\ (1-(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{1-(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y}{2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y}{2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2e} \\\\  & ≈-0.18,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-0.18.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-339695" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339695" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.18"] ["math" "-2.99"] ["math" "0.29"] ["math" "1.13"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339695" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.18"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-4x,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(2)≈$ {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-0=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-4{x}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-8{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-8{x}_{\\text{new}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-8{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ (1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-8{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y-8{x}_{\\text{new}}}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+2=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y-8{x}_{\\text{new}}}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-8\\cdot 2}{1-2\\cdot {2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{7} \\\\  & ≈2.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈2.14.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-339691" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339691" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.01"] ["math" "0.19"] ["math" "1.71"] ["math" "2.14"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339691" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.14"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(4{e}^{x}-2),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}(4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}_{\\text{new}}(4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}(4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2) & =y \\\\ (1-(4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{1-(4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3-4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3-4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3-4{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3-4e} \\\\  & ≈-0.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-0.25.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-339696" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339696" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.25"] ["math" "-2.88"] ["math" "0.39"] ["math" "1.12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339696" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.25"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y({x}^{2}+1),\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) & =y \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ≈2.67,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈2.67.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-339635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.63"] ["math" "2.33"] ["math" "2.67"] ["math" "3.44"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339635" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.67"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1-{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})+\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})+\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ (1-\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) \\\\ 2(1-\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2-3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2-3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1}{2-3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=2.2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2-3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2.2-{1}^{2}+1}{2-3\\cdot {1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-4.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈2.20,\\,y(1)≈-4.40.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-339720" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339720" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.20"] ["math" "1.50"] ["math" "2.20"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339720" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.20"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339720" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339720" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4.12"] ["math" "-4.40"] ["math" "4.33"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339720" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4.40"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x-3y,\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(0)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(0)≈$ {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(0).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=0$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-{x}_{\\text{new}}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(0).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-{x}_{\\text{new}}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1-(-\\frac{1}{2})}{5} \\\\  & =0.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot y-{x}_{\\text{new}}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0.5-0}{5} \\\\  & =0.20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(-\\frac{1}{2})≈0.50,\\,y(0)≈0.20.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-339713" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339713" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.40"] ["math" "0.50"] ["math" "1.50"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339713" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.50"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339713" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339713" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.20"] ["math" "0.20"] ["math" "1.20"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339713" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.20"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ (1+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(2y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)}{1+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1}{2+2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=1.3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1.3+{1}^{2}+1}{2+2({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =1.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.30,\\,y(1)≈1.15.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-339719" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339719" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.20"] ["math" "1.30"] ["math" "1.50"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339719" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.30"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339719" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339719" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.15"] ["math" "1.33"] ["math" "1.54"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339719" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+\\frac{1}{2})}{5} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=0.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(0.6+1)}{5} \\\\  & =0.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈0.60,\\,y(1)≈0.64.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242e