ence/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339642" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.64"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3x+y,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(0)$  rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(0)≈$ {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(0).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=0$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y-3{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(0).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y-3{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =5.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y-3{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5.5-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(-\\frac{1}{2})≈5.50,\\,y(0)≈11.00.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ce31de1618196ddea4442bbb6966b8bd568200251fed615f91ca378dfe86b2cb"} {"question" "q-339717" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339717" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5.00"] ["math" "5.50"] ["math" "5.67"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339717" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5.50"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339717" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339717" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11.00"] ["math" "6.67"] ["math" "9.50"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "q-339717" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11.00"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/state.json" "file_sha256" "ddd78df5360fc0037a20dee0f242eedb04224f759fc2b3e4e4c7af9c24cbe53d"} {"question" "e-21312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2x^2+3xy, \\qquad y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $\\Delta x = 1.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_\\text{new},$ in terms of the new value $x_\\text{new}$ and the current value $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/example-21312.json" "file_sha256" "1ced93ea9c12c67e99b6084201cf8461e0d3644d0f576e148ca33a59dd2ddc80"} {"question" "e-21312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/example-21312.json" "file_sha256" "1ced93ea9c12c67e99b6084201cf8461e0d3644d0f576e148ca33a59dd2ddc80"} {"question" "e-21318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2y + e^x, \\qquad y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/example-21318.json" "file_sha256" "e7ad5c1cb692c25c870ed22def924e43c113e2933bec8f18aa36e4c25d6f57f9"} {"question" "e-21318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac12,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12 - 0 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In our case,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1/2}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\sqrt{e}-2)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac{4}{7} \\left(\\sqrt{e} - 2\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/example-21318.json" "file_sha256" "e7ad5c1cb692c25c870ed22def924e43c113e2933bec8f18aa36e4c25d6f57f9"} {"question" "e-21661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x^2 - y, \\qquad y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $y(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/example-21661.json" "file_sha256" "d6cbc0fe72a79ac6c2bd76797d85f66d4f9bb18fbc75a4a93dfffc9f12db0cc7"} {"question" "e-21661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(4({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx = 0,\\qquad y = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(0+2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac12, \\qquad y = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac12 = 1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(\\frac{1}{3}+2\\cdot {1}^{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad\ny(1)\\approx\\dfrac{14}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046555/example-21661.json" "file_sha256" "d6cbc0fe72a79ac6c2bd76797d85f66d4f9bb18fbc75a4a93dfffc9f12db0cc7"} {"question" "q-325169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/